#!/bin/sh
# ClipNest installs system-wide files, but two of the things it needs belong to
# the user who is going to use it: enabling the shell extension for their
# account, and registering their keyboard shortcut. A root script cannot do
# either reliably, so it says so instead of pretending the package is finished.
set -e

if [ "$1" = "configure" ]; then
	cat <<'MESSAGE'

ClipNest is installed. Two steps are left, and they are per-user:

    clipnest setup       # enable the shell extension, register Win+Shift+V
    (then log out and back in once, so GNOME loads the new extension)

Until then the shortcut and the panel work, but new copies are not recorded,
because nothing is feeding them to the daemon. `/usr/share/doc/clipnest/README.md`
has the rest: settings, backup, troubleshooting.

MESSAGE
fi

exit 0
